# L §2.5 Keys: "A key literal is resolved by its position: in allowance[u5] it must be a key of allowance's
# first index or kind"; "A bare key used elsewhere (k == u5) takes the index, kind or axis of the other operand."
# x[b] reads the row for b (2); count of k in ks where k == b is 1.  The same spelling b is a key of js too.
=== m.tally
model t
  tally 2
  on 2026-01-01
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2026-12-31

source s "a source"

index ks = [a, b]
index js = [b, c]
input x[k in ks]: int

def n: int = count(k in ks where k == b)

version v
  cite s
  governs always
  known 2026-01-01
  x[a] = 1
  x[b] = 2

ask q1: x[b], n
=== expect
q1: (2, 1)
  cut 2026-01-01
